<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Nienhuys</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Nienhuys was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> tobacco planter who in 1863 began growing tobacco in the Sultanate of Deli, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, on land leased from the sultan. His success, especially with fine wrapper leaf for cigars, drew a rush of European planters to the region, and in 1869 he helped found the Deli Company. The plantation economy he started relied on Chinese and later Javanese contract labourers working under harsh conditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacob-nienhuys.jpg" alt="Jacob Nienhuys" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem Maris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nienhuys,_J,_(1836-1928).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Dutch took control in 1862, the court leased vast tracts to planters such as <b>Jacob Nienhuys</b>, whose wrapper tobacco became world famous.</blockquote><a href="/state/deli-sultanate">Deli Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Bloemendaal, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.4,4.63&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Bloemendaal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4654 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jacob Nienhuys"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5104 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5104 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.4,4.63&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
